docs: correct the diagnosis after the spatial-term sweep

The previous commit recorded "the vector candidate set collapses onto one
depth" as the dominant cause of the misses, and WHITEPAPER 9.3, 10.1, 11
and 12 all repeated it. That explanation has since been measured and is
wrong.

Scaling the spatial term from 1.0 down to 0.0 leaves R@1/R@5/R@10 at
33.3/48.3/51.7% at every scale, so the spatial term is not what buries a
strong semantic match. The extended diagnostic already showed why: all 60
answers are embedded, they score a mean cosine of 0.935, and they sit at
mean rank 6,209 of 46,565. The ranking and the embeddings are sound; the
corpus holds 36,568 D5 summaries of similar content that outscore an exact
answer, so a specific fact does not stand out in the vector space.

Sections 9.3, 10.1, 11 and 12, BENCHMARKS and the comment at the `want`
call site now say that, and record the three rejected hypotheses
(deeper pre-fetch, duplicate-vector cap, spatial scaling) with their
measured outcomes instead of an unverified mechanism. The 44 ms latency
cost previously attributed to the 64 -> 256 change is withdrawn as being
within run-to-run variance (p50 varies 331-376 ms across runs of one
build); recall figures are stable.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>